<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  RM_SeaCoast.xml                    mandrake.rm.seashores · authored 2026-09-23
  ============================================================================
  SEA_FLOOR_AND_CATCH_PASS_1 (a). The shore a land tile gets when the water it
  borders is a modded sea rather than the vanilla Ocean def.

  Shaped deliberately as a drop-in twin of vanilla's Coast
  (Data/Core/Defs/MapGeneration/TileMutators.xml): same `Coast` category, same
  genOrder 100, same priority 0. That matters twice over —
    - Tile.AddMutator removes an existing mutator sharing a category only when
      the incoming priority is >= the existing one, so an equal priority means
      this cleanly replaces a Coast/Lakeshore rather than logging a conflict;
    - WorldGenStep_Mutators asks for TileMutatorDefOf.Coast and our Harmony
      prefix substitutes this def in its place, so nothing downstream sees a
      different shape of mutator.

  🔴 coastSidesRange is deliberately LEFT UNSET. TileMutatorDef.IsValidTile
  implements it by counting neighbours whose PrimaryBiome is BiomeDefOf.Ocean
  or BiomeDefOf.Lake — the vanilla defs by reference. Any value here would
  count zero for every tile beside one of our seas and refuse the def outright.
  The equivalent gate lives in RM_TileMutatorWorker_SeaCoast.IsValidTile.

  The worker inherits all of TileMutatorWorker_Coast and overrides only the
  four answers vanilla hardcodes to the Ocean def / the land biome: the coast
  angle, and the deep / shallow / beach terrains.
-->
<Defs>

  <TileMutatorDef>
    <defName>RM_SeaCoast</defName>
    <label>sea coast</label>
    <description>This tile borders a sea.</description>
    <workerClass>RimMandrake.SeaShores.RM_TileMutatorWorker_SeaCoast</workerClass>
    <categories>
      <li>Coast</li>
    </categories>
    <genOrder>100</genOrder>
    <priority>0</priority>
  </TileMutatorDef>

</Defs>
